<h2>Psyllium husk and Mediterranean-linked seafood</h2>
<p>France's profile in our verified data is led by psyllium husk (within the broader HS 1211.90 category, so not a psyllium-specific figure) and a genuinely notable line in squid and cuttlefish — part of a broader European/Mediterranean pattern we also see in Italy and Spain. Oilcake residues, soybean meal and organic manure move in smaller volume.</p>

<h2>A note for French-speaking buyers researching Indian spices</h2>
<p>If you found this page looking for turmeric, cumin or ginger content in French, it's worth knowing our data shows Morocco — not metropolitan France — as the larger, better-established market for those specific spice lines, appearing as a top-5 destination for several of them. We've built our French-language spice content accordingly. This page reflects what genuinely moves into France itself, which is a different product mix.</p>

<h2>Two ports, two different roles</h2>
<p>Le Havre serves as France's primary Atlantic-facing gateway and the more common routing for northern-EU-bound cargo, while Marseille (Fos) on the Mediterranean coast is often the more efficient call for shipments continuing onward within southern Europe. We confirm which suits your buyer's onward distribution before booking rather than defaulting to one. Documentation follows the EU-wide TRACES pre-notification and Maximum Residue Limit framework used across all member states — the same system as our other EU pages, not a separately maintained French standard.</p>

